  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Programme for OCV to access and receive quality education through systemic chang</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">There are 4 million children of school going age in Zambia. Many of these children are unable to attend conventional schools due to high poverty levels, the impact of HIV on families, long distances (especially for girls and disabled children), lack of classroom space, and compulsory requirements for uniforms and learning materials. There has been a rapid and spontaneous development of community schools in Zambia where children are educated outside the formal school system. A community school is a community based, owned and managed learning institution that meets the basic education needs of students who cannot enter government schools. Embraced by the Zambian government, these local initiatives have grown into a national movement. Statistics show that community schools in Zambia account for the enrolment of at least 500,000 children - approximately 20% of the total basic education enrolment in the country. However, the quality of education is poor, retention rates are low, and learner achievement is weak. Many of these problems could be resolved if government legislation recognising community schools as a valid part of the education system, and thus eligible for proper government support, was fully implemented. The project will work to advocate for proper implementation of government policy with regard to community schools; to strengthen community capacity both to manage community schools and to monitor and mobilise government support for community schools; and to improve teaching and learning within community schools in three provinces of Zambia (Western, Southern and Lusaka). In addition, the project plans to strengthen the capacity of ZOCS itself both to implement the project and to participate in supporting community schools and advocating on their behalf over the long term.</narrative>
  </description>
